window.POSTS = [{"id":1010,"slug":"kyrgyzstan-travel-guide-2027","title":"Kyrgyzstan Travel Guide 2027: What to Know, Best Places, Routes & Emergency Numbers","seoTitle":"Kyrgyzstan Travel Guide 2027: Visas, Routes & Top Places","description":"Planning Kyrgyzstan in 2027? The new 30-day visa rule, best places, 5 to 14 day routes, border permits, costs and every emergency number, from locals.","category":"Travel guide","excerpt":"What we tell friends before their first trip: the new 30-day visa rule, the 16 places worth your time, routes from 5 to 14 days, costs and the numbers to call.","author":"Azat, founder & guide","authorImg":"","date":"2026-10-25","cover":"img/hero/hero-2-lake-view-2000.jpg","cover_alt":"A traveller with arms raised above Song-Kul lake","images":[],"body":"Kyrgyzstan is suddenly on everyone's list for 2027, and the rules for visiting changed in 2026. We wrote this guide as the local team in Bishkek: what to know before booking, the places worth your time, routes from 5 to 14 days and the numbers to call if something goes wrong. Save it offline, because mobile signal disappears fast in the mountains.\n\n## Why Kyrgyzstan is the place to go in 2027\n\nKyrgyzstan tops Skyscanner's list of trending destinations for 2027: return-flight searches for Bishkek from the UK rose 501% year on year, the biggest jump on the list ([Time Out](https://www.timeout.com/news/skyscanner-list-of-travel-destinations-on-the-rise-for-2027-100826), [Falstaff](https://www.falstaff.com/en/news/the-unexpected-10-travel-destinations-for-2027)). Skyscanner credits the BBC's *Race Across the World* and how close Bishkek sits to mountain trails. Searches from Australia grew even faster.\n\nThe interest is earned. More than 90% of the country is mountains, you can sleep in a shepherd's yurt at 3,000 m two days after landing, and a full week with a driver and guide still costs less than a few nights in the Alps.\n\n## Kyrgyzstan at a glance\n\n| Topic | What to know |\n| --- | --- |\n| Capital | Bishkek (main airport: Manas, FRU) |\n| Currency | Kyrgyz som (KGS), about 87 som to 1 USD in 2026 |\n| Languages | Kyrgyz and Russian. English in hotels, cafés and with guides, rarely in villages |\n| Time zone | UTC+6, no daylight saving |\n| Phone code | +996 |\n| Plugs | Type C and F, 220 V (as in continental Europe) |\n| Driving | On the right. Many cars are right-hand-drive imports |\n| Visa | Most Western passports: visa-free, 30 days in any 60 |\n| Emergency | 112 (all services) |\n| Best season | Mid-June to mid-September for the mountains, December to March for skiing ([month-by-month guide](/blog/best-time-to-visit-kyrgyzstan/)) |\n\n## What's new for travellers in 2027\n\nFive changes matter most. The first two can ruin a trip if you miss them.\n\n| Change | Since | What it means for you |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Visa-free stay cut to **30 days in any 60** for 55 countries (US, UK, all EU, Canada, Australia, NZ, Japan, Korea and more) | 1 January 2026 | Plan trips of up to 30 days or apply for an e-visa. Leaving and coming back does not reset the clock ([US Embassy](https://kg.usembassy.gov/traveling-to-the-kyrgyz-republic/), [VisasNews](https://visasnews.com/en/kyrgyzstan-shortens-visa-free-stay-for-citizens-of-55-countries/)) |\n| Kazakhstan's **QazETA** electronic travel authorization becomes mandatory | 1 July 2027 | Flying into Almaty and driving to Kyrgyzstan? Get the free QazETA before you fly, or you may be refused entry to Kazakhstan ([VisasNews](https://visasnews.com/?p=40678)) |\n| All Kyrgyz airlines removed from the EU Air Safety List after 20 years | June 2026 | Opens the door to direct flights between Kyrgyzstan and Europe, with Germany expected first ([Times of Central Asia](https://timesca.com/kyrgyzstan-airlines-see-eu-flight-ban-lifted-after-two-decades/)) |\n| More regional flights to Issyk-Kul (Tamchy airport), including seasonal Tashkent and Astana routes | Summer 2026 | Lake holidays without the 4-hour drive from Bishkek ([Qazinform](https://qazinform.com/news/kazakhstan-expands-international-air-network-with-new-flights-to-china-and-kyrgyzstan-a2863a)) |\n| Stricter drone rules: permission needed beyond basic consumer use, extra limits near protected sites | Draft rules, April 2026 | Fly a small drone only in open countryside, never near borders, airports or military sites, and ask your guide first ([24.kg](https://24.kg/english/371613_Drones_only_with_permission_Kyrgyzstan_introduces_strict_flight_rules/)) |\n\nWhat has **not** changed yet is the long-discussed single Central Asian tourist visa. Kyrgyzstan revived the plan in 2026, but there is no launch date, price or portal ([VisasNews](https://visasnews.com/en/kyrgyzstan-revives-central-asia-single-visa-plan-with-support-from-kazakhstan-and-uzbekistan/)). Plan each country's entry separately.\n\n## Visa, entry rules and border permits\n\nWith a Western passport and a stay under 30 days, you need nothing but a passport valid for the trip. Under Cabinet Resolution No. 855, in force since 31 December 2025, the visa-free stay depends on nationality ([VisasNews](https://visasnews.com/en/kyrgyzstan-shortens-visa-free-stay-for-citizens-of-55-countries/)):\n\n| Passport | Visa-free stay |\n| --- | --- |\n| 55 countries including the USA, UK, all EU, Switzerland, Norway, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, Japan, South Korea, Singapore, Brazil, Mexico and Israel | 30 days within any 60-day period |\n| Saudi Arabia, UAE, Qatar, Kuwait, Oman, Bahrain | 180 days within 360 |\n| Turkey, Serbia, Ukraine, Mongolia | 90 days within 180 |\n| Russia, Kazakhstan, Armenia, Belarus (EAEU) | 90 days within 180 |\n| Uzbekistan | 60 days within 180 |\n| Everyone else | E-visa through the official portal [evisa.e-gov.kg](https://www.evisa.e-gov.kg/) |\n\nOverstaying is costly: you pay a fine and need an exit visa, which cannot be sorted out at the airport ([US Embassy](https://kg.usembassy.gov/traveling-to-the-kyrgyz-republic/)). Staying longer than 30 days? Apply for an e-visa before you arrive and register your stay within 5 days, which your host or hotel helps with. Use only the official portal, because look-alike sites charge extra for the same e-visa. And if you arrive through Almaty after 1 July 2027, add the free QazETA to your checklist.\n\n### Border zone permits\n\nSome of the most spectacular places lie in the border zone, up to roughly 50 km from the borders with China and Tajikistan. You need a permit from the State Border Service, and guards check it with your passport at checkpoints. Order it at least 7 to 10 working days ahead; it is valid for about a month. Tour operators, us included, arrange it from a passport scan.\n\n| Region | Places that need a permit |\n| --- | --- |\n| Issyk-Kul (east) | Enylchek (Inylchek) glacier and ghost town, Merzbacher Lake, Khan Tengri base camp, Sary-Jaz valley, Karkyra, Ak-Shyirak |\n| Naryn | Kel-Suu (Kol-Suu) lake, Ak-Sai valley, Chatyr-Kul lake, Torugart pass |\n| Osh and Alay | Lenin Peak base camp (Achik-Tash), Tulpar-Kul lake |\n| Chui | Parts of the Chon-Kemin valley |\n| Batken | Southern valleys (separate permit) |\n\nPopular places that do **not** need a permit: Song-Kul, Ala-Archa, Karakol and its valleys (Altyn-Arashan, Ala-Kul, Jeti-Oguz), Tash Rabat, Sary-Chelek, Arslanbob and the whole Issyk-Kul shoreline.\n\n## Getting there and getting around\n\nMost visitors fly into Bishkek's Manas airport through Istanbul, Dubai, Almaty or Tashkent. Osh is the gateway to the south and the Pamir Highway. For connections and arrival logistics, see [our guide to travelling from abroad](/blog/easy-from-abroad/).\n\n| Route | How | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Almaty → Bishkek | Shared taxi, bus or private transfer, about 4 h including the border | The cheapest long-haul fares often land in Almaty. Check QazETA from July 2027 |\n| Manas airport → Bishkek centre | Yandex Go taxi or a pre-booked transfer, 30 to 40 min | Avoid unofficial drivers in the arrivals hall |\n| Bishkek → Issyk-Kul | Shared taxi or minibus from the Western bus station, or the summer train to Balykchy | The train runs June to mid-September through the scenic Boom gorge |\n| Bishkek → Karakol | Minibus (7 to 8 h) or a private car with driver (6 to 7 h) | Combine with south-shore stops: Skazka, Barskoon, Jeti-Oguz |\n| Bishkek → Osh | Domestic flight (1 h) or a 10 to 12 h drive over two passes | The drive is spectacular, the flight saves a day |\n| Song-Kul, Kel-Suu, Tash Rabat | 4x4 with driver | Gravel roads, river crossings, no public transport |\n\n**Our advice:** in cities use Yandex Go, and between regions hire a car with a local driver. Self-drive rentals exist, but mountain roads, livestock and night driving cause most accidents involving visitors.\n\n## Money, SIM cards and what things cost\n\nCarry cash in som outside the cities. Cards work in hotels and restaurants in Bishkek, Osh, Karakol and Cholpon-Ata, while yurt camps, guesthouses, bazaars and shared taxis take cash only.\n\n**Cash.** Bring clean USD or EUR notes and change them at banks or licensed exchange offices in Bishkek, where rates are fair and commission is usually zero. ATMs are common in towns and rare in villages.\n\n**SIM card.** Buy a local SIM (Beeline, MegaCom or O!) at the airport or in a branch with your passport; a month of data costs a few dollars, and an eSIM works too. Coverage is good on main roads and around Issyk-Kul, patchy or absent at Song-Kul, Ala-Kul and in the high valleys.\n\n**Offline maps.** Download Maps.me or Organic Maps, plus Google Maps offline areas, before you leave Bishkek.\n\n| Item | Typical 2026 price (approximate) |\n| --- | --- |\n| Ala-Archa park entry | 200 som per person, cable car about 600 som ([A Social Nomad](https://asocialnomad.com/?p=67183)) |\n| Meal in a city café | 5 to 10 USD |\n| Guesthouse with breakfast | 20 to 40 USD per person |\n| Yurt camp with dinner and breakfast | 25 to 45 USD per person |\n| Horse rental per day | 20 to 30 USD, guide extra |\n| Private car with driver, long-distance day | 80 to 150 USD per car |\n\nWe publish what our own tours include and why in [our fair-pricing post](/blog/fair-honest-pricing/).\n\n## The 16 most popular places in Kyrgyzstan\n\nWith one week, choose Issyk-Kul, Karakol and Song-Kul. With two, add Naryn, Tash Rabat and either Kel-Suu or the south.\n\n### Bishkek and the north\n\n**1. Bishkek.** A green Soviet-era capital with snow peaks at the end of its avenues. Walk Ala-Too Square, the Osh Bazaar and Panfilov Park, and eat at a Kyrgyz or Uzbek chaikhana. One day is enough, and our [city tour](/tours/bishkek-soviet-heritage-city-tour-day-trip/) packs it into a morning and an afternoon.\n\n**2. Ala-Archa National Park.** The easiest mountains in the country, 40 km south of Bishkek. Private petrol cars no longer drive in; park buses take you to the trailheads. The easy walk is the waterfall trail (3 to 4 h return), the hard one is the climb to the Ratsek hut at 3,350 m with glacier views. We run it as a [day hike at your pace](/tours/ala-archa-gorge-hike-at-your-pace-day-trip/).\n\n[img:img/catalog/own-f7c1a3f621d0.jpg|Granite peaks above the Ala-Archa gorge, 40 km from Bishkek]\n\n**3. Burana Tower.** An 11th-century minaret near Tokmok, a stop on the road to Issyk-Kul, with a field of balbal stone figures. An hour is enough, or make it a [day trip with Bishkek](/tours/silk-road-burana-tower-bishkek-day-trip/).\n\n[img:img/catalog/own-a63d2b4403a0.jpg|Burana Tower, the 11th-century minaret near Tokmok]\n\n**4. Chon-Kemin valley.** A quiet green valley with guesthouses, horse riding and rafting, 2.5 h from Bishkek. Part of the upper valley is in the border zone.\n\n### Issyk-Kul lake\n\n**5. Issyk-Kul north shore (Cholpon-Ata).** Beaches, resorts and the open-air petroglyph museum. Busy in July and August.\n\n[img:img/catalog/own-0502741a5ad0.jpg|Cholpon-Ata on the north shore of Issyk-Kul]\n\n**6. Issyk-Kul south shore.** Wilder and quieter: yurt camps on the beach at Bokonbaevo and Tamga, eagle-hunting demonstrations and the Barskoon waterfalls.\n\n[img:img/catalog/own-965068e8428f.jpg|Red cliffs and an empty beach on the quieter south shore]\n\n**7. Skazka (Fairy Tale) Canyon.** Red and orange sandstone ridges a short drive from the lake near Tosor. Best in the late afternoon light; there is a small cash entry fee.\n\n[img:img/catalog/own-505f6522d8aa.jpg|The red sandstone ridges of Skazka canyon]\n\n**8. Jeti-Oguz (Seven Bulls).** Red cliffs shaped like seven bulls and the Broken Heart rock, 25 km from Karakol. Continue to the Valley of Flowers for a day hike.\n\n[img:img/catalog/own-e1c69253a306.jpg|The red cliffs of the Jeti-Oguz gorge]\n\n**9. Karakol.** The trekking capital: the wooden Holy Trinity cathedral, the Dungan mosque built without nails, the Sunday animal market and ashlyan-fu noodles.\n\n[img:img/catalog/own-ae70d7e7d82e.jpg|The wooden Dungan mosque in Karakol]\n\n**10. Altyn-Arashan and Ala-Kul.** Hot springs in a pine valley, then a 3,900 m pass to a turquoise glacier lake. It is the classic 3 to 4 day trek of Kyrgyzstan; read our [full Ala-Kul trek guide](/blog/ala-kol-trek-guide/).\n\n[img:img/catalog/own-fb294b877406.jpg|The turquoise Ala-Kul lake above Karakol]\n\n### Central Kyrgyzstan and Naryn\n\n**11. Song-Kul lake.** A high-pasture lake at about 3,000 m where shepherd families live in yurts from June to September. Ride a horse, drink kymyz, watch the stars. Our [yurt etiquette guide](/blog/yurt-culture-etiquette/) tells you how to be a good guest, and the [3-day horse trek](/tours/song-kul-horse-trek-3-days/) is the classic way in.\n\n[img:img/catalog/own-d55a1aaaf052.jpg|Yurts on the shore of Song-Kul]\n\n**12. Kochkor.** A village base for Song-Kul and the home of shyrdak felt carpets, where you can watch a felt-making workshop. Community tourism started here ([how it helps families](/blog/community-tourism/)).\n\n[img:img/catalog/own-a76bfd751021.jpg|Kyrgyz felt craft: yurts and camels in pressed wool]\n\n**13. Tash Rabat.** A 15th-century stone caravanserai on the Silk Road at 3,200 m, with yurts nearby and a ride towards Chatyr-Kul.\n\n[img:img/places/tash-rabat.jpg|Tash Rabat caravanserai in its green valley]\n\n**14. Kel-Suu (Kol-Suu) lake.** A narrow lake between sheer cliffs near the Chinese border. It needs a 4x4, a border permit and a boat or a walk. Remote and unforgettable; our [Kel-Suu Lake Express](/tours/kel-suu-lake-express-4-days/) does it in 4 days.\n\n[img:img/places/kel-suu.jpg|Kel-Suu between its sheer cliffs]\n\n### The south\n\n**15. Osh and Sulaiman-Too.** One of Central Asia's oldest cities, with the UNESCO-listed sacred mountain and one of the biggest bazaars in the region. The start of the Pamir Highway and the road to Lenin Peak.\n\n**16. Arslanbob and Sary-Chelek.** Arslanbob has the world's largest walnut forest and waterfalls; Sary-Chelek is a deep blue lake in a biosphere reserve. Both work as a 3 to 4 day add-on from Osh or Bishkek.\n\n## Best Kyrgyzstan routes for 2027: 5 to 14 days\n\nAll four routes start and end in Bishkek, fit inside the 30-day visa-free stay and run best from mid-June to mid-September. Driving times assume a private car.\n\n### 5 days: Issyk-Kul loop\n\n| Day | Route | Highlights | Night |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Bishkek → Ala-Archa → Bishkek | Waterfall hike, city walk | Bishkek |\n| 2 | Bishkek → Burana → Cholpon-Ata (4 h) | Burana Tower, petroglyphs, swim | Cholpon-Ata |\n| 3 | Cholpon-Ata → Karakol (2.5 h) | Cathedral, Dungan mosque, ashlyan-fu | Karakol |\n| 4 | Karakol → Jeti-Oguz → Skazka → Bokonbaevo (4 h) | Seven Bulls, Fairy Tale Canyon, eagle show | Yurt camp |\n| 5 | Bokonbaevo → Bishkek (4 h) | Lakeside road, Boom gorge | Bishkek |\n\n### 7 days: Lakes and nomads\n\n| Day | Route | Highlights | Night |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Arrival, Bishkek | Osh Bazaar, Ala-Too Square | Bishkek |\n| 2 | Bishkek → Kochkor → Song-Kul (6 h) | Felt workshop, first yurt night | Yurt, Song-Kul |\n| 3 | Song-Kul | Horse ride, shepherd life, stars | Yurt, Song-Kul |\n| 4 | Song-Kul → Bokonbaevo (5 h) | Kalmak-Ashuu pass, eagle hunter | Yurt camp by the lake |\n| 5 | Bokonbaevo → Skazka → Jeti-Oguz → Karakol | Canyons, red rocks | Karakol |\n| 6 | Karakol → Cholpon-Ata | Altyn-Arashan day trip or the beach | Cholpon-Ata |\n| 7 | Cholpon-Ata → Bishkek | Burana Tower stop | Flight home |\n\nPrefer it arranged? Our [Easy Week: Song-Kul & Issyk-Kul](/tours/easy-week-song-kul-issyk-kul-7-days/) covers much of this route with a driver and guide.\n\n### 10 days: Classic Kyrgyzstan with Tash Rabat\n\nFollow the 7-day route to Song-Kul, then go south. Day 4 Song-Kul to Naryn, day 5 the Tash Rabat caravanserai with a yurt night, day 6 Tash Rabat to Kochkor or Bokonbaevo, days 7 to 9 the south shore, Jeti-Oguz and Karakol with a hot-spring night in Altyn-Arashan, and day 10 back to Bishkek. The closest ready-made version is our [Tash-Rabat & Kel-Suu Road Trip](/tours/tash-rabat-kel-suu-road-trip-11-days/), one day longer.\n\n### 14 days: North and south\n\nDays 1 to 10 as above, then fly from Bishkek to Osh on day 11 (Sulaiman-Too, the bazaar), spend days 12 and 13 in the Arslanbob walnut forest and at its waterfalls, and on day 14 fly back or drive the whole road to Bishkek through Toktogul and over the Too-Ashuu pass. If you prefer wilderness to the south, add Kel-Suu instead (2 days, permit needed).\n\n### Treks for hikers\n\n| Trek | Days | Max altitude | Level | Permit |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Ala-Archa to the Ratsek hut | 1 or 2 | 3,350 m | Moderate | No |\n| Karakol, Ala-Kul and Altyn-Arashan | 3 or 4 | about 3,900 m | Moderate to hard | No |\n| Song-Kul on horseback from Kochkor | 3 | about 3,400 m | Easy to moderate | No |\n| Keskenkyia loop (Jyrgalan) | 4 or 5 | about 3,600 m | Moderate | No |\n| Enylchek glacier to Khan Tengri base camp | 8 to 12 | about 4,000 m | Hard, expedition | Yes |\n| Lenin Peak base camp (Achik-Tash) | 2 or 3 | 3,600 m | Moderate | Yes |\n\nWant a different pace or season? We build [tailor-made trips](/blog/tailor-made-trips/) around your dates.\n\n## Safety, etiquette and what to pack\n\nKyrgyzstan is a low-crime country for visitors; the real risks are roads, altitude and weather. Our [mountain safety guide](/blog/safety-in-the-mountains/) goes deeper.\n\n**Roads.** Avoid driving after dark, when livestock, unlit trucks and potholes are hard to see, and wear seat belts.\n\n**Altitude.** Song-Kul, Tash Rabat and Ala-Kul are at 3,000 to 3,900 m. Climb slowly, drink water, and go down if a headache gets worse.\n\n**Weather.** Snow can fall on the passes even in July. Never trek alone, and tell your guesthouse your route and when you plan to be back.\n\n**Water.** Drink bottled or boiled water, because streams below the pastures carry livestock bacteria.\n\n**Police checks.** Carry your passport, or a copy with the original close by. Fines are paid at a bank or against a receipt, never in cash on the spot.\n\n**Insurance.** Buy travel insurance that covers trekking up to 4,000 m and helicopter evacuation.\n\n**Etiquette in short:** take off your shoes in yurts and homes, accept tea with your right hand, ask before photographing people, and dress modestly at mosques and in the south. Full details are in our [yurt culture guide](/blog/yurt-culture-etiquette/).\n\n### Packing checklist\n\n- Passport valid for the whole trip, plus 2 photocopies\n- Border permit printout, if your route needs one\n- Warm layers and a down jacket: yurt nights drop near 0 °C even in August\n- Waterproof jacket and broken-in hiking boots\n- Sunscreen SPF 50, sunglasses and a hat (strong UV at altitude)\n- Headlamp and power bank (no electricity in many yurt camps)\n- Basic first-aid kit, altitude and stomach medicine\n- Cash in som, in small notes\n- Offline maps and this page saved\n\n## Emergency numbers and where to get help\n\nIn any emergency dial **112**. It is the unified number run by the Ministry of Emergency Situations and it also handles mountain rescue ([UK FCDO](https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/kyrgyzstan/getting-help), [MChS](https://www.mchs.gov.kg/en/news/osh-kutkaruuchular-turistke-zhardam-berishti/)). Operators speak Kyrgyz and Russian and English is limited, so ask your host or guide to call with you. Save these numbers and write them on paper too.\n\n| Service | Number | When to call |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unified emergency, including mountain rescue | **112** | Any threat to life, an accident in the mountains, a lost hiker |\n| Fire | 101 | Fire |\n| Police | 102 | Theft, assault, road accident |\n| Ambulance | 103 | Medical emergency |\n| Gas emergency | 104 | Gas leak ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telephone_numbers_in_Kyrgyzstan)) |\n| Tourist police call centre, Issyk-Kul region (English, Turkish, Russian, Kyrgyz) | +996 705 009 102 | Problems at the lake, scams, lost documents ([24.kg](https://24.kg/english/238373_Round-the-clock_call_center_in_four_languages_opened_for_tourists_in_Issyk-Kul)) |\n| U.S. Embassy Bishkek | +996 312 597 000 | Lost passport, arrest, serious incident (US citizens) |\n| Other embassies | Your government's travel advice page | UK: [FCDO Kyrgyzstan](https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/kyrgyzstan/getting-help), Canada: [travel.gc.ca](https://travel.gc.ca/assistance/embassies-consulates/kyrgyzstan) |\n\nIn the summer season the Interior Ministry puts English-speaking tourist police on duty in Bishkek, Osh and the Issyk-Kul region; one recent season counted 146 such officers ([24.kg](https://24.kg/english/326168_Unified_security_service_to_work_during_tourist_season_in_Kyrgyzstan)).\n\n### What to do if…\n\n1. **Someone is hurt in the mountains.** Call 112 and give your location (share GPS coordinates from Maps.me), stay together and keep the injured person warm. With no signal, send one person to the nearest yurt or ranger post.\n2. **Your passport is lost or stolen.** Report it at the nearest police station (102) and get a written report, then contact your embassy for an emergency travel document.\n3. **You are about to overstay.** Go to the State Registration Service in Bishkek before your 30 days end; leaving late means a fine and an exit visa.\n4. **You feel unwell at altitude.** Go down at least 500 m. Karakol, Naryn and Osh have regional hospitals, and serious cases go to Bishkek.\n\nTravelling with us? Your guide is with you on the route, and you can always reach us on WhatsApp at +996 502 888 001.\n\n## Plan your 2027 trip with locals\n\nAzat Tours is a Bishkek team of [real local guides](/blog/real-local-guides/). We handle border permits, drivers, yurt stays and the route, and you get [honest, itemised prices](/blog/fair-honest-pricing/). Tell us your dates and pace, and we send a day-by-day plan within 24 hours.","faq":[{"q":"Do I need a visa for Kyrgyzstan in 2027?","a":"Most Western travellers don't. Citizens of 55 countries, including the US, UK, EU, Canada and Australia, can stay visa-free for 30 days within any 60-day period. Others apply online at evisa.e-gov.kg."},{"q":"Is Kyrgyzstan safe for tourists?","a":"Yes. Violent crime against visitors is rare. The main risks are mountain roads, altitude and sudden changes in the weather, so travel with a local driver and never trek alone."},{"q":"What is the emergency number in Kyrgyzstan?","a":"Dial 112 for all emergencies, including mountain rescue. Police is 102, ambulance 103, fire 101."},{"q":"How many days do I need in Kyrgyzstan?","a":"Seven days covers Issyk-Kul, Karakol and a night at Song-Kul. Ten to fourteen days adds Tash Rabat, Naryn and the south around Osh."},{"q":"When is the best time to visit?","a":"Mid-June to mid-September for yurt stays and treks, with July and August the warmest months. Our month-by-month guide on this blog goes into each season."},{"q":"Do I need a permit for Kel-Suu, Khan Tengri or Lenin Peak?","a":"Yes, all three are in the border zone. Order the permit through a tour operator 7 to 10 working days before you go."},{"q":"Can I pay by card?","a":"In Bishkek, Osh and Karakol, mostly yes. In villages, yurt camps and markets you need cash in som."},{"q":"I'm flying into Almaty. Anything new?","a":"From 1 July 2027, visa-exempt travellers need Kazakhstan's free QazETA authorization before entering Kazakhstan."}],"status":"published","updatedAt":"2026-10-25"},{"id":1003,"slug":"ala-kol-trek-guide","title":"The complete guide to the Ala-Kul trek","category":"Travel guide","excerpt":"Route day by day, the easier and the harder direction, season, costs, where to sleep and what to pack for the Ala-Kul trek, by local guides.","author":"Azat, founder & guide","authorImg":"","date":"2026-06-02","cover":"img/blog/ala-kol-trek-guide.jpg","images":[],"body":"Ask any traveller what they remember most about Kyrgyzstan and there's a good chance they'll say one word: Ala-Kul. This glacier-fed lake glows an unreal turquoise at 3,560 m, and reaching it is the country's most loved multi-day trek. Here's everything you need to walk it well.\n\n## The trek at a glance\n\n- Route: Karakol valley, Sirota yurt camp, Ala-Kul lake and pass, Altyn-Arashan, Ak-Suu\n- Length: about 55 km, walked in 3 to 4 days\n- Highest point: Ala-Kul pass, about 3,900 m\n- Season: mid-June to mid-September\n- Park ticket: 400 som, no permit needed\n\n## Where is Ala-Kul and why go?\n\nAla-Kul sits high in the Terskey Ala-Too range above Karakol, on the eastern side of Lake Issyk-Kul. The classic route climbs through pine forest and alpine meadow, crosses a 3,900 m pass with jaw-dropping views, then drops to the natural hot springs of Altyn-Arashan.\n\n[img:img/blog/body/ala-kol-pass.jpg|The pass day rewards every step with a panorama over the Tian Shan.]\n\n## Which way to walk it\n\nThere are two ways to do this trek, and the choice changes how hard it feels.\n\n**The easier way** is to start from Karakol. You drive into the Karakol valley, walk up past the Sirota yurt camp to the lake, cross the pass and come down through Altyn-Arashan. The climb is spread over two days, and the steepest slope of the whole route is one you walk down, not up.\n\n**The harder way** is to go up and come back down on the Altyn-Arashan side. It is a little tougher because the climb from Altyn-Arashan to the pass is sharp. What makes it popular is the horses: you can ride from Altyn-Arashan to the foot of the final climb in about 3 hours, roughly an hour and a half faster than hiking it, and save your legs for the pass.\n\nYou do not have to walk into Altyn-Arashan either. Horses or a bukhanka, the old Soviet UAZ van that handles the rough valley track, can take you up or bring you down. Write to us and we will arrange either.\n\n## The route, day by day\n\nThis is the easier direction, at the pace most people walk it.\n\n### Day 1: Karakol valley to the first camp\n\nAbout 15 km and four to five hours along the river, on a wide track through forest and open meadow. It is a gentle start, and you sleep near the water.\n\n### Day 2: Up past Sirota to Ala-Kul lake\n\nThe shortest day on the map, about 10 km, and the hardest on the legs. Above the Sirota yurt camp the path climbs steeply over roots, rock and gravel. Then the ground levels and the lake is suddenly below you. The night by the water is cold and often windy.\n\n### Day 3: Over the pass to Altyn-Arashan\n\nThe big one, about 15 km and a long day. You walk along the lake, climb to the pass at about 3,900 m and get the view everyone comes for. The far side is a steep slope of loose scree, and it is the part people find hardest. Poles help, and so does patience. The valley opens out below, and the day ends in a hot pool at Altyn-Arashan.\n\n### Day 4: Altyn-Arashan to Ak-Suu\n\nAbout 15 km down the valley, mostly downhill on a rough track. If your knees have had enough, this is where the horses or the bukhanka come in.\n\n## How hard is it?\n\nHonestly? It's a proper trek, moderate to hard. You don't need technical skills, but you do need reasonable fitness and the willingness to spend a long day above 3,500 m. The height is what catches people out: a headache and a poor night's sleep at the lake are common.\n\n[tip:Local tip|Spend a night in Karakol and do a short acclimatisation walk before the pass day. It makes a huge difference to how you feel up high.]\n\n## The best time to walk it\n\nThe trail is reliably open and snow-light from **mid-June to mid-September**. July and August are warmest and busiest; early September brings golden light and thinner crowds. Even in midsummer the weather turns fast up there, so pack for sun, rain and cold on the same day.\n\n> The lake doesn't look real the first time you see it. People go quiet. Then they start taking a hundred photos.\n\n## What it costs\n\nYou do not need a permit. The entrance ticket to Karakol national park is **400 som**. On the route, expect about **2,500 som for a bed** and about **900 som for dinner**. Bring cash in som, because nobody up there takes cards.\n\n## Where to sleep\n\nIn season there are yurt camps on the Karakol side, including the one at Sirota, and guesthouses in Altyn-Arashan next to the hot springs. Our pick in Altyn-Arashan is the Elza guesthouse: after the pass day, a warm room and a hot pool a few steps away is exactly what you want. You can also camp, if you carry your own tent, mat and stove.\n\n## What to pack\n\nPack for four seasons. Broken-in hiking boots, trekking poles, a warm layer and a down jacket, a waterproof shell, thermals for the night at the lake, a hat and gloves, sunglasses and sunscreen, a headlamp, a way to filter or boil water and a power bank. Add swimwear for the hot springs.\n\n## Can I do it independently?\n\nYou can, and some do. But weather windows, river crossings and the pass demand respect, and there's no phone signal up there. A local guide handles logistics, food and the all-important \"is today a good day for the pass?\" call, so you can simply enjoy it.\n\n## Walk it with us\nWant a local guide, packhorses and a hot-spring finish? The pass is part of our [Song-Kul horse trek and Ala-Kul hike (7 days)](/tours/song-kul-horse-trek-ala-kul-hike-7-days/) and of the [Kyrgyzstan Grand Tour (10 days)](/tours/kyrgyzstan-grand-tour-10-days/). If the climb sounds like too much, the [Kilemche, Song-Kul and Altyn-Arashan trip (6 days)](/tours/kilemche-song-kul-altyn-arashan-6-days/) treks the gentler Altyn-Arashan valley instead. Only need horses or a ride to Altyn-Arashan? [Send us a message](/contact.html) and we will set it up.","updatedAt":"2026-10-13","faq":[{"q":"How long is the Ala-Kul trek?","a":"About 55 km in total, usually walked in 3 or 4 days between the Karakol valley and Ak-Suu."},{"q":"How high is the Ala-Kul pass?","a":"About 3,900 m. The lake just below it is at about 3,560 m."},{"q":"When is the best time to do the Ala-Kul trek?","a":"From mid-June to mid-September. July and August are the warmest and busiest months, and early September is quieter."},{"q":"Do I need a permit for Ala-Kul?","a":"No. You only buy an entrance ticket to Karakol national park, which costs 400 som."},{"q":"Which direction is easier?","a":"Starting in the Karakol valley, climbing past the Sirota yurt camp and coming down through Altyn-Arashan. Going up from the Altyn-Arashan side is harder because that slope is much steeper."},{"q":"Can I ride a horse on the Ala-Kul trek?","a":"Yes, on the Altyn-Arashan side. Riding from Altyn-Arashan to the foot of the final climb takes about 3 hours, roughly an hour and a half faster than walking it."},{"q":"How much does a night on the trek cost?","a":"Expect about 2,500 som for a bed and about 900 som for dinner at the camps and guesthouses."}]},{"id":1002,"slug":"best-time-to-visit-kyrgyzstan","title":"When is the best time to visit Kyrgyzstan?","category":"Planning","excerpt":"Month-by-month: when to trek, ride, see the lakes or ski in Kyrgyzstan.","author":"Azat, founder & guide","authorImg":"","date":"2026-05-20","cover":"img/blog/best-time-summer.jpg","images":[],"body":"Short answer: **June to September** for the mountains, and you won't be disappointed. But Kyrgyzstan has a season for almost everyone, here's how to match your dates to the trip you actually want.\n\n## Summer (June-September): the classic season\n\nThis is prime time. High passes are open, the jailoo (summer pastures) are green and dotted with yurts, and herder families move up into the mountains.\n\n- Best for: trekking, horse treks, yurt stays, Issyk-Kul, Kel-Suu\n- Weather: warm days, cold nights; afternoon showers possible\n- Note: July-August are busiest, book ahead\n\n[img:img/blog/body/summer-jailoo.jpg|Summer is when nomad families move up to the jailoo.]\n\n## Spring & autumn (April-May, October)\n\nThe shoulder seasons are underrated. High passes may still be snowy, but the lower valleys, Issyk-Kul shore and cultural sights are beautiful and far quieter.\n\n[tip:Sweet spot|Early September is many guides' favourite, summer access, warm lakes, golden colours and fewer people.]\n\n## Winter (November-March): snow & silence\n\nWinter is for a different kind of traveller. Trekking passes close, but you can ski quiet powder and ride horses to a frozen Song-Kul.\n\n- Best for: skiing, winter yurt stays, photography\n- Note: some mountain roads close\n\n## Match the season to a trip\nComing in summer? Ride to [Song-Kul on horseback (3 days)](/tours/song-kul-horse-trek-3-days/) while the jailoo is green. From November to April we run our only [winter ride to a frozen Song-Kul (3 days)](/tours/winter-horse-trek-to-song-kul-3-days/). For the shoulder months, the [Issyk-Kul road trip (4 days)](/tours/issyk-kul-altyn-arashan-4-days/) runs from April right into November.","updatedAt":"2026-10-25"},{"id":1001,"slug":"yurt-culture-etiquette","title":"Staying in a yurt: nomad culture & etiquette","category":"Culture","excerpt":"The culture behind the Kyrgyz yurt, what to expect, and how to be a respectful guest.","author":"Azat, founder & guide","authorImg":"","date":"2026-05-08","cover":"img/blog/yurt-camp.jpg","images":[],"body":"A night in a yurt is the moment most travellers fall in love with Kyrgyzstan. It's not a hotel gimmick here, it's a living home that has sheltered nomad families on these pastures for centuries.\n\n## More than a tent\n\nThe Kyrgyz **boz üy** is a feat of nomadic engineering: a round lattice frame, a felt cover, and a wooden crown, the tündük, so important it sits at the centre of the national flag.\n\n[img:img/blog/body/yurt-closeup.jpg|The felt-covered boz üy, a home that packs onto a horse.]\n\n## What a night is really like\n\nExpect comfort that's simple and rich at the same time. You'll sleep on thick felt mattresses under warm blankets, often with a wood stove. Facilities are basic, usually a shared outdoor toilet and limited signal. That disconnection is the point.\n\n- Meals are home-cooked and generous\n- You may be offered kymyz (fermented mare's milk)\n- Mornings start with the sounds of animals\n\n> \"A guest brings happiness,\" goes the Kyrgyz saying. Hospitality isn't a service here, it's an honour.\n\n## Simple etiquette to be a great guest\n\n- Accept food and tea when offered, even just a little\n- Take bread seriously, don't waste it or place it upside down\n- Use your right hand to give and receive\n- Ask before photographing people\n- Dress modestly\n\n[tip:A small gesture|Bringing a little gift, sweets for the kids, or tea, is a lovely way to say thank you.]\n\n## Sleep in one, not just read about it\nEvery Song-Kul trip stays with real herder families. Start small with [Song-Kul on horseback (2 days)](/tours/song-kul-overnight-horse-trek-2-days/), or live the daily rhythm on the [authentic horseback tour (5 days)](/tours/mai-tor-pastures-horse-trek-5-days/) with yurt and shepherd-house nights.","updatedAt":"2026-10-25"},{"id":1009,"slug":"real-local-guides","category":"Travelling with us","date":"2026-04-28","title":"Why a real local guide changes the whole trip","excerpt":"Not a script-reading rep, a Kyrgyz guide who knows the passes, the families and the safe river crossings.","author":"Azat, founder & guide","authorImg":"","cover":"img/blog/local-guide-eagle.jpg","body":"On paper, a guide is a guide. In the mountains of Kyrgyzstan, the difference between a local one and the rest is the difference between watching a country and being let into it.\n\n## We grew up in these mountains\n\nOur guides are Kyrgyz. We learned these valleys on horseback before we ever called it work, which pass clears first in summer, which family keeps a yurt by which lake, which river is calm in the morning and dangerous by afternoon.\n\nThat kind of knowledge does not come from a training course. It comes from moving sheep up to the jailoo as a kid, from riding to a neighbour's camp for tea, from being caught out by mountain weather enough times to respect it. I have been guiding since 2019, and the honest truth is that most of what makes a trip work I learned long before that.\n\n[img:img/blog/body/guide-riders.jpg|A pass looks different to someone who has crossed it a hundred times.]\n\n## What a local guide actually does for you\n\n- Reads the weather and the passes, and changes the plan when the mountains say so\n- Knows which river crossing is safe today, not in general\n- Speaks Kyrgyz and Russian, so doors, and yurts, open\n- Knows the families along the route, because we have been stopping at the same camps for years\n- Turns a viewpoint into a story, and a stranger's tent into tea and bread\n\n## A day with a guide, hour by hour\n\nSay we are riding toward Song-Kul. Before breakfast your guide has already looked at the sky, felt the wind and decided whether today is the day for the pass or the day for the lakeshore. Over tea he tells you the plan, and why. On the trail he rides where he can see everyone, adjusts stirrups, swaps horses if a pairing is not working. At the river he goes first. In the evening he is the one talking with the host family in Kyrgyz, which is why dinner can end in stories and laughter around the stove, not with a bill.\n\nNone of that shows up on an itinerary PDF. All of it is the trip.\n\n## The doors a local opens\n\nKyrgyz hospitality is real, but it opens fully when someone the family trusts makes the introduction. Arriving with a local guide means you are a guest, not a customer. You notice it in small things: the seat you are given, the questions you are asked, the bowl of kymyz (fermented mare's milk, the jailoo welcome drink) offered whether you asked for it or not.\n\nThis matters even more at the remote camps. The herders we stay with are our partners year after year, and that relationship is the reason the welcome feels nothing like a hotel check-in. More on how that works in [where your money stays](/blog/community-tourism/).\n\n## Local knowledge is a safety system\n\nThe most useful safety equipment in these mountains is judgement. Weather shifts fast above 3,000 m, rivers rise through the day as snow melts, and a pass that was fine on Tuesday can be a bad idea on Wednesday. A guide who grew up here makes those calls early and calmly, long before they become problems. The whole system, vehicles, first aid, satellite comms, is in [how we look after you](/blog/safety-in-the-mountains/).\n\n## Horses matched to riders\n\nIf your trip involves horses, your guide is also the one matching each horse to each rider. Complete beginners get the steady characters and a gentle first day; confident riders get horses that like to move. Nobody gets a surprise. It is why people who have never ridden finish a multi-day trek feeling like they gained a skill, not survived an ordeal.\n\n[tip:Ask us anything|Your guide is the same person before, during and after the trip, message us and a real local answers, not a call centre.]\n\n> Travellers always say the same thing at the end: the mountains were unforgettable, but it was the people who made it home.\n\nThis is the whole idea behind Azat Tours. \"Azat\" means free in Kyrgyz, and we want you to feel free here, and at home.\n\n## Meet them on the trail\n\nOur guides shine brightest on the long rides: a full week in the saddle on the [horseback riding tour (7 days)](/tours/two-lakes-kok-bulak-horse-trek-7-days/), far off the map on the [untouched mountains ride (6 days)](/tours/ichke-tor-lake-horse-trek-6-days/), or the big mix of riding and trekking from [Song-Kul to Ala-Kul (7 days)](/tours/song-kul-horse-trek-ala-kul-hike-7-days/).","images":[],"updatedAt":"2026-10-25"},{"id":1008,"slug":"tailor-made-trips","category":"Travelling with us","date":"2026-04-26","title":"Tailor-made by default: your trip, your pace","excerpt":"Tell us your dates, pace and budget. We design an itinerary that fits you, not rigid package tourism.","author":"Azat, founder & guide","authorImg":"","cover":"img/blog/tailor-made-trips.jpg","body":"We don't sell seats on a fixed conveyor belt. Every trip we run starts from a blank page and three questions: when can you come, how hard do you want to walk, and what do you actually want to feel?\n\n## No two travellers are the same\n\nA family with kids, a couple who want long days in the saddle, a photographer chasing light, the same region, three completely different trips. Our published tours are a starting point, not a cage.\n\n## The questions behind the questions\n\n\"When can you come\" is really a season question: July means green jailoo and lively camps, September means golden light and quiet trails. \"How hard\" is an honesty question: four hours in a saddle feels very different from four hours on a sofa, and there is no prize for suffering on holiday. \"What do you want to feel\" is the one that shapes everything. Some people come for the wide-open silence, some for the horses, some for the family table in a village. Tell us that, and the route almost draws itself.\n\n## How tailoring works, step by step\n\n- Tell us your dates, pace and budget, roughly is fine\n- We reply within 24 hours with a route that fits the season and your time\n- We adjust together until it feels right: add a rest day, swap a trek for a horse ride, trade a hotel night for a yurt night\n- You travel privately or in a small group, and the plan stays flexible on the ground\n\n## Say you have nine days\n\nHere is how the thinking goes. Nine days in July with two riders who want saddle time: we build around Song-Kul, ride camp to camp for the middle stretch, and keep the first and last days light for arrival and the flight home. The same nine days with two kids under ten becomes a different trip: shorter drives, guesthouses with warm showers, a gentle one-day ride instead of a five-day trek, more time at the Issyk-Kul shore. Same country, same length, built around different people.\n\n## What we can change, and what we cannot\n\nHonesty works both ways, so here is the other side. We can shape pace, comfort level, activities and route order almost endlessly. We cannot change the mountains: high passes open roughly June to September, Song-Kul yurt camps fold in early October, and some drives are simply long because Kyrgyzstan is big. If your dates fall outside the season for the thing you want most, we will say so and offer the honest alternative. The full season picture is in [when to visit](/blog/best-time-to-visit-kyrgyzstan/).\n\n## Changes on the ground\n\nTailoring does not stop when the trip starts. Your guide checks in as you go: sleeping well in the yurt, or want a guesthouse tomorrow? Legs tired, or ready for the longer trail? We build routes with slack in the right places precisely so these choices exist. If the group falls in love with a lake, we can often stay the extra night and shorten a drive later. The plan is a tool, not a contract carved in stone.\n\n## Private or small group\n\nMost tailored trips run privately: you, your people, your guide and driver. If you would rather share the road and the campfire, tell us, small groups happen when travellers with similar plans and dates combine. Either way the group never gets big, because half of what we offer is flexibility, and flexibility dies at bus scale.\n\n[tip:Free to ask|Planning is free and there's no prepayment. We only want you on a trip that's right for you.]\n\n## What it costs\n\nA tailored trip is not automatically an expensive one. The price follows your choices: season, group size, comfort level, activities. A September trip in guesthouses costs less than a July trip with a full camp crew. We quote transparently and itemise what is included; the whole approach is in [fair, honest pricing](/blog/fair-honest-pricing/).\n\n> The best itinerary isn't the one with the most stops. It's the one that still has room to say \"let's stay here another night.\"\n\nSend us a rough idea and we'll turn it into a real plan within 24 hours.\n\n## Start shaping yours\n\nUse the [trip builder](/builder.html) to sketch your dates and interests, or take a base like the [Complete Kyrgyzstan Adventure (12 days)](/tours/complete-kyrgyzstan-adventure-12-days/) and bend it to fit. Short on time? [Kyrgyzstan in a week (7 days)](/tours/song-kul-kel-suu-in-a-week-7-days/) packs the icons into seven days, and most of our routes exist in several lengths, so the days can flex too.","images":[],"updatedAt":"2026-10-25"},{"id":1007,"slug":"safety-in-the-mountains","category":"Travelling with us","date":"2026-04-24","title":"Safety first: how we look after you in the mountains","excerpt":"Vetted vehicles, first-aid-trained guides, satellite comms in remote areas and a 24/7 team back in Bishkek.","author":"Azat, founder & guide","authorImg":"","cover":"img/blog/safety-in-the-mountains.jpg","body":"Kyrgyzstan's mountains are wild in the best way, high passes, remote valleys, real rivers. We love that. It's also exactly why safety isn't an afterthought for us; it's built into how every trip runs.\n\n## Two different kinds of safe\n\nFirst, the country itself. Kyrgyzstan is one of Central Asia's most stable and welcoming destinations, rated Level 1 by the US State Department, \"exercise normal precautions\", and solo and women travellers consistently find it comfortable. Crime against visitors is rare; in Bishkek the advice is the same as in any city, watch your pockets in busy spots like Osh Bazaar.\n\nThe real risks here are different: weather, altitude, rivers and long mountain roads. Those are exactly the risks a good operator can manage, and that is what the rest of this page is about. The country-level picture lives in our [travel guide](/plan-trip.html#safety).\n\n## Safety starts at the planning table\n\nThe cheapest place to fix a problem is before it exists. When we design your route we match it to your actual fitness and riding experience, not to how the photos look. If a route is wrong for your group we say so and propose the version that fits, that is half the value of [tailoring](/blog/tailor-made-trips/). We also build weather slack into itineraries in the seasons that need it, so a stormy day costs you a view, not the whole plan.\n\n## On the road\n\n- Vehicles are checked and maintained, no tired vans on mountain switchbacks\n- Drivers know these roads in every season, including which stretches to take slowly\n- Distances are planned honestly: we would rather add a stop than push a driver through a marathon day\n\n## On the trail\n\n- Guides are first-aid trained and carry a kit\n- In remote areas we carry satellite communication, because phone signal disappears up high\n- Routes build in acclimatisation, and we make honest calls: if a pass isn't safe today, we wait or reroute\n\n[tip:The \"is today a good day?\" call|Weather and rivers change fast. A local guide making that decision is the single biggest safety factor on any trek.]\n\n## Altitude, respected\n\nMuch of what you came to see sits above 3,000 m: Song-Kul's pastures, Ala-Kul's turquoise water at 3,560 m, passes near 3,900 m. Altitude is not something to fear, it is something to respect with time. Our higher routes climb gradually, first days stay moderate, and your guide watches how everyone feels. Your part: drink more water than feels necessary, tell your guide early if your head aches, and do not treat day one as a race. The full health picture, including why travel insurance with mountain cover is required on our tours, is in the [guide](/plan-trip.html#health).\n\n## Rivers and horses\n\nMountain rivers rise through the day as snow melts, so a crossing that was easy at nine can be serious at four. Local guides plan crossings for the right hour and go first. On horse treks, every rider gets a horse matched to their level, beginners get the calm veterans, and the first hours are gentle on purpose. The horses know these trails better than any of us.\n\n## Small things that matter\n\n- Drink bottled or treated water when off-grid; we keep supplies stocked\n- Sun at altitude is stronger than it feels: high-SPF sunscreen and a hat are not optional\n- Shepherd dogs guard the herds along some trails; your guide knows how to pass them calmly\n- Mountain weather does all four seasons in a day, pack layers, the [packing list](/plan-trip.html#packing) has details\n\n## Someone is always reachable\n\nThere's a 24/7 support team back in Bishkek for the whole trip, for you and for the people at home. Remote camps have satellite contact, so \"off the grid\" never means \"out of reach\".\n\n## What we ask of you\n\nSafety is a partnership, so we ask a few things in return: be honest about fitness and health when we plan (there is no wrong answer, only wrong routes), bring travel insurance with mountain cover, and listen to your guide on the trail, especially about rivers, weather and altitude.\n\n> Adventure and safety aren't opposites. Done right, feeling safe is what lets you actually relax and enjoy the wild.\n\n## Comfortable first steps\n\nIf you want the mountains with a soft landing, the [family tour (4 days)](/tours/family-tour-lakes-eagles-yurts-4-days/) and the gentle [easy week by road (7 days)](/tours/easy-week-song-kul-issyk-kul-7-days/) keep the wild scenery and skip the hard passes. Ready for the classic high trek instead? Read the [complete Ala-Kul guide](/blog/ala-kol-trek-guide/) first.","images":[],"updatedAt":"2026-10-25"},{"id":1006,"slug":"community-tourism","category":"Travelling with us","date":"2026-04-22","title":"Community tourism: where your money stays","excerpt":"We partner with herder families and village hosts, so your money stays in the mountains it came from.","author":"Azat, founder & guide","authorImg":"","cover":"img/blog/community-tourism.jpg","body":"Tourism can either drain a place or feed it. We've chosen the second one, on purpose, and from the start.\n\n## Who you actually stay with\n\nWhen you sleep in a yurt by Song-Kul or eat a home-cooked dinner in a village, that's a real family's home and livelihood, not a chain. We work with herder families and village hosts directly.\n\nThe rhythm behind it is old. Every May, families move their animals up to the jailoo, the high summer pastures, and set up camp; every September they come back down. Hosting a few travellers fits into that life without replacing it. The horses you ride are working horses, the milk in your tea came from the herd outside, and the family's year still turns around the animals, not around tourism.\n\n[img:img/blog/body/summer-pasture.jpg|Summer pastures come alive when families move up with their animals, and a few guests.]\n\n## What \"direct\" actually means\n\nNo layers. We agree terms with the families themselves, season by season, and pay them directly for every guest night and every meal. Our guides and drivers are local, hired here and paid here. We won't dress that up with invented percentages, we simply built the company so there is no chain of resellers between your payment and the people doing the work.\n\n## The other model, briefly\n\nYou have seen it elsewhere: tours sold abroad, profits landing abroad, locals cast as scenery. The mountains end up with the litter and not much else. We are not neutral about this, we live here. Azat Tours exists partly so that the money Kyrgyzstan's mountains attract actually reaches the mountains, and every booking is a small vote for that model.\n\n## Why it matters\n\n- Your payment reaches the families and guides doing the work\n- Income stays in mountain villages, helping them keep the nomadic way of life alive\n- Young people get a reason to stay in the valleys instead of leaving for the city\n- You get something no resort can sell: a genuine welcome\n\n## What a stay is actually like\n\nExpect a felt-lined yurt, thick blankets, a table that fills with bread, jam, cream and tea the moment you sit down, and a silence at night you may never have heard before. Comfort is real but honest: beds are warm, the toilet is usually a short walk away, showers are a town thing. We wrote a whole guide to it, [staying in a yurt](/blog/yurt-culture-etiquette/), including the etiquette that makes hosts light up.\n\n## Small groups, on purpose\n\nA yurt camp can host a handful of guests without changing what it is. A bus cannot arrive at a family camp without turning it into a stage. That is one of the quiet reasons we keep groups small: it keeps the exchange human on both sides, and it is why the welcome you get is real rather than rehearsed.\n\n[tip:Be a good guest|A small gift, sweets for the kids, or tea, and a little curiosity go a long way. Hospitality here is an honour, not a transaction.]\n\n## How to leave a place better\n\n- Carry your litter down, everything you pack in comes back out\n- Ask before photographing people, a smile and a gesture is enough\n- Buy local where you can: felt crafts, honey, kurut (the salty dried-yoghurt balls you will be offered on every jailoo, accept at least one, they grow on you)\n- Learn three words of Kyrgyz, \"rakhmat\" (thank you) opens more doors than you would think\n- Respect the water: streams here are drinking water for people and herds\n\n## When to come\n\nThe full jailoo experience runs May to September, while families are up on the pastures; July and August are the liveliest. September is quieter and golden, and the last camps at Song-Kul fold in early October. The season-by-season picture is in [when to visit](/blog/best-time-to-visit-kyrgyzstan/).\n\n> \"A guest brings happiness,\" goes the Kyrgyz saying. Travel here in a way that leaves the place better, and you'll feel the difference both ways.\n\n## Trips built around local homes\n\nThe [authentic road trip (8 days)](/tours/talas-to-issyk-kul-backroads-8-days/) sleeps in family guesthouses and nomad camps most tours never reach, the [untouched mountains ride (6 days)](/tours/ichke-tor-lake-horse-trek-6-days/) stays with herders the whole way, and the short [Song-Kul on horseback (3 days)](/tours/song-kul-horse-trek-3-days/) is the classic first taste of jailoo life.","images":[],"updatedAt":"2026-10-25"},{"id":1005,"slug":"easy-from-abroad","category":"Travelling with us","date":"2026-04-20","title":"Easy from abroad: visas, arrival and logistics","excerpt":"Visa-free for most Western and East-Asian passports. We help with airport pickup, SIM cards, logistics and everything in between.","author":"Azat, founder & guide","authorImg":"","cover":"img/blog/easy-arrival.jpg","body":"Kyrgyzstan is far easier to visit than most people expect. Half the worry of a trip is the getting-there part, so we take that off your plate.\n\n## Visas: simpler than you think\n\nTravellers from the EU, UK, USA, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, Japan and South Korea enter visa-free for **up to 30 days within any 60-day period**. This changed at the end of 2025, and plenty of older guides online still say 60 days, so check your own nationality on evisa.e-gov.kg (rules checked July 2026). Staying longer? The online Sapar e-visa covers up to 90 days. Tell us your passport and we'll confirm exactly what applies to you.\n\nFor a one- or two-week trip, which is what most of our travellers do, the honest summary is: with most Western and East-Asian passports there is nothing to arrange at all. Land, get stamped, start.\n\n## Getting here: one stop, usually\n\nThere is no single long-haul hub into Bishkek yet, so nearly everyone connects once. The common routes:\n\n- Istanbul (Turkish Airlines, Pegasus), the busiest option from Europe and the Americas\n- Dubai (flydubai) from the Gulf and beyond\n- Almaty (Air Astana) from much of Asia\n- Osh (OSS) in the south, handy if your route starts near the Pamir or the Fergana valley\n\nFlying long-haul into Almaty in Kazakhstan and crossing overland is sometimes cheaper; the border is a 3 to 4 hour drive from Bishkek. It is not for everyone, ask us and we will tell you honestly whether it makes sense for your route.\n\n[tip:Just land|Most travellers arrive into Bishkek (Manas, code BSZ since August 2025, formerly FRU). Send us your flight and someone will be waiting with your name.]\n\n## We meet you at the airport\n\n- Airport pickup, day or night, long-haul connections tend to land at unfriendly hours and we plan for it\n- A local SIM card so you're connected from the start\n- Help with city logistics, money and your first night\n\nArrival night is part of the trip design, not an afterthought: if you land at 4 a.m., the first day's plan respects that, breakfast late, start gently.\n\n## Money, without the guesswork\n\nThe currency is the Kyrgyz som, around 87 to 90 to the US dollar in 2026. The working rules:\n\n- Cards work in Bishkek hotels and supermarkets, rarely in villages\n- Carry cash for the mountains; ATMs live in cities and towns, mostly take Visa, and often cap withdrawals around 20,000 som\n- Wise and Revolut cards withdraw som with low fees\n- On our multi-day tours most costs are covered anyway, you mainly need cash for drinks, souvenirs and tips (appreciated, never required)\n\n## Staying connected\n\nCities and main roads have solid 4G; remote camps and high passes have blissful nothing. A local SIM or eSIM costs about 300 to 500 som for plenty of data. Download offline maps before heading up, and warn the people at home that two quiet days at a yurt camp are part of the plan, not a problem. Operator advice is in the [travel guide](/plan-trip.html#connectivity).\n\n## What to pack, in one paragraph\n\nLayers, always layers: mountain weather does sun, wind and rain in one afternoon, and evenings are cold even in July. Broken-in boots for treks, a power bank because yurt camps have little electricity, and EU-style sockets (type C/F, 220V) everywhere else. The full list, down to the sleeping-bag liner, is in the [packing guide](/plan-trip.html#packing).\n\n## Everything in between\n\nTransfers, domestic logistics, the route, the guesthouses, the guide, it's all arranged before you arrive, so your trip starts the moment you step out of the airport. You will not be negotiating taxis at dawn or guessing which marshrutka goes to the mountains. That is our job.\n\n## Timing your trip\n\nJune to September is the headline season, when the high country is open. If your dates are fixed, tell us and we will shape the route to the season; if they are flexible, [when to visit](/blog/best-time-to-visit-kyrgyzstan/) breaks the year down month by month.\n\n> You handle the flights. We'll handle Kyrgyzstan.\n\n## Land, ride, done\n\nBoth of these start and end in Bishkek and pack the country into one simple loop: [Kyrgyzstan in a week (7 days)](/tours/song-kul-kel-suu-in-a-week-7-days/) and the faster [express tour (5 days)](/tours/kel-suu-issyk-kul-express-5-days/). Questions first? The full [Kyrgyzstan travel guide](/plan-trip.html) answers the eight most common ones.","images":[],"updatedAt":"2026-10-25"},{"id":1004,"slug":"fair-honest-pricing","category":"Travelling with us","date":"2026-04-18","title":"Fair, honest pricing, no hidden extras","excerpt":"Transparent quotes, no hidden extras, and a best-price promise. Enquire free, pay only when you are ready.","author":"Azat, founder & guide","authorImg":"","cover":"img/blog/fair-honest-pricing.jpg","body":"Nobody likes a price that grows after you've said yes. So ours don't.\n\n## What a quote includes\n\nWhen we send you a price, it's the real one: guide, transport, accommodation, the agreed activities, and most meals on multi-day routes. We spell out what's in and what's not, line by line, so there are no surprises in the mountains. The usual out-of-pocket list is short: drinks, souvenirs and tips, and tips are appreciated, never required.\n\n## Why our tours say \"price on request\"\n\nFair question: why not print one big number on every tour page? Because one number would be wrong for most people. The same nine-day route costs different money for two people and for six, in July and in September, in guesthouses and with a full camp crew. A package price has to be padded to cover every case; a personal quote only has to cover yours. So we quote per trip: tell us dates and group, and you get the real number within 24 hours, itemised.\n\n## How a quote is actually built\n\nNo mystery. A trip is transport (vehicle, fuel, driver days), guiding, nights (guesthouse, yurt camp, hotel), activities (horses come with their own logistics, a horseman travels with the group), and food. We price each part from current local rates for your dates, and our work carries one margin on top, not a markup at every layer. When you see the itemised quote you can move the sliders yourself: fewer vehicle days, more nights in one place, a different mix of camps and guesthouses.\n\n## How we keep it fair\n\n- Transparent quotes, itemised so you can see where your money goes\n- No hidden extras bolted on later: what we agreed is what you pay\n- A best-price promise, because we work direct, not through layers of middlemen\n- Local economics: our guides, drivers and host families are paid here, fairly, which is leaner than an imported supply chain and better for the valleys, see [where your money stays](/blog/community-tourism/)\n\n[tip:No prepayment to ask|Enquiring is free and you don't pay to plan. You only commit when the trip is right.]\n\n## Tell us your budget, really\n\nThe most useful sentence in trip planning is an honest budget. For us it is not a negotiating position, it is a design constraint. Tight budget? September dates, guesthouses over camps, or a shorter variant of the same route, most of our routes exist in several lengths, the [overland road trip](/tours/kyrgyzstan-overland-road-trip-7-days/) alone runs from 7 to 12 days. Room to spare? We will tell you where extra money genuinely buys a better trip, a rest day, a more comfortable vehicle for the long drives, and where it would just buy padding.\n\n## What \"no hidden extras\" means in practice\n\nChanges on the ground happen: weather closes a pass, the group falls in love with a lake. When plans shift, we talk about any cost difference before it happens, not on the last evening. If a change saves money, that comes back to you too. The rule is boring and simple: no surprises in either direction.\n\n## Currency and paying\n\nQuotes come in US dollars because that is what most travellers compare in; on the ground the currency is the Kyrgyz som, about 87 to 90 to the dollar in 2026. How and when to pay is agreed per trip, and enquiring costs nothing, there is no deposit just to hold a conversation. The practical money side of travelling here, cash, cards, ATMs, tipping, is covered in the [travel guide](/plan-trip.html#money).\n\n## Tailored, not inflated\n\nBecause every trip is custom, the price reflects your group size, season and choices, not a padded package rate. And value here is honest to both sides: fair for you, and fair for the families and guides doing the work. Cheap at someone else's expense is not a deal we sell.\n\n> Fair pricing is really just respect. Tell us your budget honestly and we'll tell you honestly what's possible.\n\n## See it in practice\n\nEvery tour page lists exactly what's included, day by day: compare the [Song-Kul and Kel-Suu road trip (9 days)](/tours/song-kul-kel-suu-road-trip-9-days/) with the compact [Song-Kul ride and Altyn-Arashan trek (5 days)](/tours/song-kul-ride-altyn-arashan-trek-5-days/) and you'll see the same open approach on both.","images":[],"updatedAt":"2026-10-25"}];
